hadiths[36]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2169","Anas ibn Malik reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""I was brought the Buraq, a white animal, long, larger than a donkey but smaller than a mule. It would place its hoof at the farthest point of its sight. So I rode it until I reached Bayt al-Maqdis (Jerusalem). Then I tethered it to the ring where the Prophets tether their mounts. Then I entered the mosque and prayed two rak'ahs therein. Then I came out, and Gabriel (peace be upon him) brought me a vessel of wine and a vessel of milk. I chose the milk. Gabriel said: 'You have chosen the Fitrah (natural disposition).' Then we ascended to the heaven.Gabriel asked for the gate to be opened. It was said: 'Who are you?' He said: 'Gabriel.' It was said: 'And who is with you?' He said: 'Muhammad.' It was said: 'Has he been sent for?' He said: 'He has been sent for.' So it was opened for us. There I found Adam. He welcomed me and supplicated for me with good.Then we ascended to the second heaven. Gabriel asked for the gate to be opened. It was said: 'Who are you?' He said: 'Gabriel.' It was said: 'And who is with you?' He said: 'Muhammad.' It was said: 'Has he been sent for?' He said: 'He has been sent for.' So it was opened for us. There I found the two maternal cousins, Jesus son of Mary and Yahya son of Zakariya (peace be upon them both). They welcomed me and supplicated for me with good.Then I was ascended to the third heaven. Gabriel asked for the gate to be opened. It was said: 'Who are you?' He said: 'Gabriel.' It was said: 'And who is with you?' He said: 'Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).' It was said: 'Has he been sent for?' He said: 'He has been sent for.' So it was opened for us. There I found Yusuf (peace be upon him). He had been given half of beauty. He welcomed me and supplicated for me with good.Then we ascended to the fourth heaven. Gabriel asked for the gate to be opened. It was said: 'Who is this?' He said: 'Gabriel.' It was said: 'And who is with you?' He said: 'Muhammad.' It was said: 'Has he been sent for?' He said: 'He has been sent for.' So it was opened for us. There I found Idris (peace be upon him). He welcomed me and supplicated for me with good. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: 'And We raised him to a high station' (Maryam 19:57).Then we ascended to the fifth heaven. Gabriel asked for the gate to be opened. It was said: 'Who is this?' He said: 'Gabriel.' It was said: 'And who is with you?' He said: 'Muhammad.' It was said: 'Has he been sent for?' He said: 'He has been sent for.' So it was opened for us. There I found Harun (peace be upon him). He welcomed me and supplicated for me with good.Then we ascended to the sixth heaven. Gabriel asked for the gate to be opened. It was said: 'Who is this?' He said: 'Gabriel.' It was said: 'And who is with you?' He said: 'Muhammad.' It was said: 'Has he been sent for?' He said: 'He has been sent for.' So it was opened for us. There I found Musa (peace be upon him). He welcomed me and supplicated for me with good.Then I was ascended to the seventh heaven. Gabriel asked for the gate to be opened. It was said: 'Who is this?' He said: 'Gabriel.' It was said: 'And who is with you?' He said: 'Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).' It was said: 'Has he been sent for?' He said: 'He has been sent for.' So it was opened for us. There I found Ibrahim (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) leaning his back against the Frequented House (al-Bayt al-Ma'mur), into which seventy thousand angels enter every day, never to return to it again.Then he took me to the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary (Sidrat al-Muntaha). Its leaves were like the ears of elephants, and its fruit was like large water jars. When it was covered by what covered it of the Command of Allah, it changed, so that none of Allah's creation could describe its beauty. Then Allah revealed to me what He revealed. He made fifty prayers obligatory upon me every day and night.I descended to Musa (peace be upon him). He said: 'What did your Lord enjoin upon your Ummah?' I said: 'Fifty prayers.' He said: 'Return to your Lord and ask Him for reduction, for your Ummah will not be able to bear that. I have tested the Children of Israel and dealt with them.' So I returned to my Lord and said: 'O my Lord, lighten the burden on my Ummah.' He reduced five for me. I returned to Musa and said: 'He reduced five for me.' He said: 'Your Ummah will not be able to bear that. Return to your Lord and ask Him for reduction.' So I kept returning between my Lord, Blessed and Exalted, and Musa (peace be upon him) until He said: 'O Muhammad! They are five prayers every day and night. For every prayer there are ten [rewards], so that is fifty prayers. Whoever intends a good deed but does not do it, a good deed is recorded for him. If he does it, ten are recorded for him. Whoever intends an evil deed but does not do it, nothing is recorded against him. If he does it, one evil deed is recorded.'I descended until I reached Musa (peace be upon him) and informed him. He said: 'Return to your Lord and ask Him for reduction.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'I said: I have returned to my Lord until I felt shy before Him.'"""
"2170","[AI-translation] From Aishah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said to me: ""You were shown to me in a dream twice, and a man was carrying you in a piece of silk, saying: 'This is your wife.' So I say: If this is from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, He will bring it to pass."
"2171","On the authority of Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud, that Abdullah ibn Abbas informed him that Abu Sufyan ibn Harb informed him: that Heraclius sent for him in a delegation of Quraysh, and they were merchants in Syria during the period in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had a truce with Abu Sufyan and the disbelievers of Quraysh. So they came to him while they were in Jerusalem (Ilya'). He called them into his court, and around him were the great men of Rome. Then he called them and called for his translator. He said: ""Which of you is closest in lineage to this man who claims to be a Prophet?"" Abu Sufyan said: ""I said: 'I am the closest of them in lineage to him.'"" He said: ""Bring him close to me and bring his companions close, and make them stand behind his back."" Then he said to his translator: ""Tell them: 'I am going to ask this man about him; if he lies to me, then belie him.' By Allah! Were it not for the shame that they would attribute a lie to me, I would have lied about him.""Then the first thing he asked me about him was: ""How is his lineage among you?"" I said: ""He is of noble lineage among us."" He said: ""Has anyone among you ever said this claim before him?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Was there a king among his forefathers?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Do the nobles of the people follow him, or the weak?"" I said: ""Rather, the weak follow him."" He said: ""Are they increasing or decreasing?"" I said: ""Rather, they are increasing."" He said: ""Does anyone apostatize from his religion out of displeasure after entering it?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Did you use to accuse him of lying before he said what he said?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Does he betray?"" I said: ""No. And we are in a truce with him, but we do not know what he will do during it."" He said: ""I could not find a word to insert anything other than this word."" He said: ""Have you fought him?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""How was your fighting with him?"" I said: ""The war between us and him is alternating; he inflicts casualties on us and we inflict casualties on him."" He said: ""What does he command you?"" I said: ""He says: 'Worship Allah alone and do not associate anything with Him, and abandon what your fathers say.' And he commands us to pray, speak the truth, be chaste, and maintain family ties.""Then he said to the translator: ""Say to him: 'I asked you about his lineage, and you mentioned that he is of noble lineage among you. Thus are the Messengers sent among the lineage of their people. I asked you: Has anyone among you said this claim? You mentioned: No. I said: If someone had said this claim before him, I would have said: A man imitating a saying said before him. I asked you: Was there a king among his forefathers? You mentioned: No. I said: If there had been a king among his forefathers, I would have said: A man seeking his father's kingdom. I asked you: Did you accuse him of lying before he said what he said? You mentioned: No. Thus I know that he would not leave off lying to people and then lie to Allah. I asked you: Did the nobles follow him or the weak? You mentioned: The weak followed him, and they are the followers of the Messengers. I asked you: Are they increasing or decreasing? You mentioned: They are increasing. And thus is the affair of faith until it is completed. I asked you: Does anyone apostatize out of displeasure for his religion after entering it? You mentioned: No. And thus is faith when its delight enters the hearts. I asked you: Does he betray? You mentioned: No. And thus the Messengers do not betray. I asked you: What does he command you? You mentioned: He commands you to worship Allah and not associate anything with Him, and forbids you from worshipping idols, and commands you to pray, speak the truth, and be chaste. If what you say is true, he will possess the place of these two feet of mine. And I knew that he would appear, but I did not think he would be from you. If I knew that I would reach him, I would have exerted myself to meet him, and if I were with him, I would wash his feet.'""Then he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) which Dihyah had brought to the Great Man of Busra, who handed it to Heraclius. He read it, and it contained: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad, the slave of Allah and His Messenger, to Heraclius, the Great of Rome. Peace be upon him who follows the guidance. To proceed: I invite you with the invitation of Islam. Accept Islam and you will be safe; Allah will give you your reward twice. But if you turn away, then upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin (peasants/subjects). 'O People of the Scripture, come to a word that is equitable between us and you - that we will not worship except Allah and not associate anything with Him and not take one another as lords instead of Allah.' But if they turn away, then say, 'Bear witness that we are Muslims.'"" (Surah Ali 'Imran: 64).Abu Sufyan said: ""When he finished what he said and finished reading the letter, there was a great commotion around him, and the voices rose, and we were expelled. I said to my companions when we were expelled: 'The matter of the son of Abu Kabshah has become prominent; indeed, the King of the Children of the Yellow (Romans) fears him.' I continued to be certain that he would prevail until Allah entered Islam into my heart.""Ibn al-Natur was the owner of Jerusalem and Heraclius's viceroy over the Christians of Syria. He narrated that Heraclius, when he arrived in Jerusalem, woke up one day in a bad mood. Some of his patriarchs said: ""We have found your condition strange."" Ibn al-Natur said: Heraclius was a soothsayer who looked at the stars. He said to them when they asked him: ""I saw last night, when I looked at the stars, that the King of the Circumcised has appeared. Who among this nation practices circumcision?"" They said: ""None practice circumcision except the Jews. So do not let their matter concern you, and write to the cities of your kingdom to kill whoever is among them of the Jews."" While they were on this matter, a man was brought to Heraclius, sent by the King of Ghassan, informing about the news of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). When Heraclius questioned him, he said: ""Go and see: is he circumcised or not?"" They looked at him and informed him that he was circumcised. He asked him about the Arabs, and he said: ""They practice circumcision."" Heraclius said: ""This is the kingdom of this nation that has appeared.""Then Heraclius wrote to a friend of his in Rome who was his peer in knowledge. And Heraclius set out for Homs. He did not stay long in Homs until a letter came to him from his friend agreeing with Heraclius's opinion on the emergence of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and that he is a Prophet. Heraclius then permitted the great men of Rome into a palace of his in Homs. Then he ordered its doors to be locked. Then he appeared to them and said: ""O assembly of Romans! Do you desire success and right guidance, and that your kingdom remain firm? Then pledge allegiance to this Prophet."" They rushed towards the doors like wild donkeys, but found them locked. When Heraclius saw their aversion and despaired of their faith, he said: ""Return them to me."" And he said: ""I said my previous statement only to test your firmness on your religion. I have seen it."" So they prostrated to him and were pleased with him. That was the end of Heraclius's affair."
"2172","[AI-translation] From Aishah who said: It is written in the Gospel regarding the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family: ""He is neither harsh nor severe, nor a shouter in the markets. He does not repay an evil deed with its like, but rather pardons and forgives."
"2173","On the authority of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿlā ibn Murrah, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I saw three things from the Prophet ﷺ that no one had seen before me:A. I was with him on the road to Mecca when he passed by a woman with a son who had a severe affliction (lamam), the like of which I had never seen. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah! This is my son, as you see?"" He said: ""If you wish, I will supplicate for him?"" So he supplicated for him, then went on.B. Then he passed by a camel with its girth loose, groaning. He said: ""Bring me its owner."" He said: ""This one says: I was born among them and they worked me until I grew old, then they wanted to slaughter me."" Then he went on.C. Then he saw two trees standing apart. He said to me: ""Go and command them to join together."" So they joined together. He relieved himself, then said: ""Go and tell them to separate."" Then he went on. When he returned, he passed by a boy playing with other boys, and his mother had prepared six rams and gifted him two. She said: ""Nothing of the affliction has returned to him."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is nothing that does not know that I am the Messenger of Allah, except the disbelievers and the wicked among the jinn and mankind."""
"2174","[AI-translation] Ansari: When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, ate or drank, he would say: ""Praise is to Allah who fed, gave drink, made it easily swallowable, and made for it an exit."
"2175","[AI-translation] Khudri: When he, peace be upon him and his family, sat, he would sit with his legs folded (ihtiba')."
"2176","[AI-translation] From Ibn Mas'ud, who said: When he, peace be upon him and his family, got angry, his eyes would become red."
"2177","[AI-translation] Khudri who said: When he, peace be upon him and his family, disliked something, we would recognize it in his face."
"2178","[AI-translation] From Anas bin Malik: When he, peace be upon him and his family, walked, it was as if he were leaning."
"2179","[AI-translation] From Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him: ""When he, peace be upon him and his family, walked, he would not turn to look back."
"2180","[AI-translation] Sahl bin Sa'd said: I heard Zaid bin Thabit say: When revelation descended upon him, peace be upon him and his family, he would feel heavy because of it, and his forehead would drip with sweat like pearls, even if it were in the cold."
"2181","[AI-translation] From Aisha who said: I was asked, ""What did the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, do in his house?"" She said: He was a human among humans; he would mend his garment, milk his sheep, and serve himself."
"2182","[AI-translation] Awfi who said: I asked Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri about the seal of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, so he said: ""The seal of prophethood on his back was a raised piece of flesh."
"2183","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, was white, as if he were molded from silver, and had straight hair."
"2184","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah: He, peace be upon him, was broad in the forearms, thick-lashed, broad between the shoulders, turning his whole body when facing and when turning away. He was neither obscene nor vulgar, nor loud in the markets."
"2185","[AI-translation] From Abdullah bin Mas'ud: He, peace be upon him and his family, had a donkey called Ufayr."
"2186","[AI-translation] From Ziyad bin Sa'd that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, would not return after three."
"2187","[AI-translation] Harithi who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, used to sit in the Qurfusa posture."
"2188","[AI-translation] From Aisha who said: He, peace be upon him and his family, used to be guarded until this verse was revealed: ""And Allah will protect you from the people"" (Al-Ma'idah 67). So the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, put his head out of the tent and said to them: ""O people! Depart, for Allah has protected me."
"2189","[AI-translation] From Ibn Abbas: He, peace be upon him and his family, would walk in a manner by which it was known that he was neither weak nor lazy."
"2190","[AI-translation] From Ibn 'Umar: The most frequent oath of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was: ""No, by the Turner of hearts."
"2191","[AI-translation] From Ibn Buraydah from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When we reached Bayt al-Maqdis, Jibril pointed with his finger, pierced the stone with it, and tethered al-Buraq to it."
"2192","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When it was the night I was taken on the Night Journey, and I woke up in Mecca, I was terrified by my affair and knew that the people would belie me. So I sat aside, grieving. Then the enemy of Allah, Abu Jahl, passed by and came until he sat with him. He said to him, mockingly: ""Was there anything?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Yes."" He said: ""What is it?"" He said: ""I was taken on a journey tonight."" He said: ""To where?"" He said: ""To Bayt al-Maqdis."" He said: ""Then you woke up among us?"" He said: ""Yes."" He did not show that he belied him, fearing that he would deny the hadith if he called his people to it. He said: ""What do you think, if I call your people, will you tell them what you told me?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Come, O people of Banu Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy!"" The gatherings rushed to him and they came until they sat with them both. He said: ""Tell your people what you told me."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""I was taken on a journey tonight."" They said: ""To where?"" He said: ""To Bayt al-Maqdis."" They said: ""Then you woke up among us?"" He said: ""Yes."" So some of them were clapping (in disbelief), and some were putting their hands on their heads in amazement at the lie, they claimed. They said: ""And can you describe the mosque for us?"" And among the people were those who had traveled to that land and seen the mosque. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""So I went to describe it, and I kept describing it until some of the description became confused to me."" He said: ""Then the mosque was brought and I was looking at it until it was placed before the house of 'Iqal — or 'Uqayl — so I described it while looking at it."" He said: ""And with this was a description I did not memorize."" He said: ""The people said: As for the description, by Allah! He has indeed been accurate."""
"2193","[AI-translation] From 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud who said: We were in the campaign of Badr, and every three of us were on a camel. 'Ali and Abu Lubabah were the two riding partners of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family). When it was the Prophet's (peace be upon him and his family) turn [to walk], they said: ""Ride, O Messenger of Allah, so that we may walk instead of you."" But he would say: ""You two are not stronger in walking than I am, and I am not in less need of reward than you two."
"2194","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: I do not give you anything, nor do I withhold anything from you; I am but a treasurer; I place where I am commanded."
"2195","[AI-translation] From Ibn Buraydah from his father, raising it: No one has been harmed as I have been harmed for the sake of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"2196","[AI-translation] From 'A'ishah who said: He was not taken (in death) until Allah had made lawful for him to marry from the women whomever he willed."
"2197","[AI-translation] From 'A'ishah who said: He (peace be upon him and his family) never struck a servant or a woman with his hand, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) never struck anything with his hand ever, except to strive in the path of Allah. He was never given a choice between two matters except that the most beloved of them to him was the easiest of them, unless it was a sin. If it was a sin, he was the furthest of people from the sin. And he never sought revenge for himself for anything done to him, until the sacred limits of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, were violated, in which case he would seek revenge for Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"2198","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, he said: The chiefs of Quraysh passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) while with him were Ṣuhayb, Bilāl, ʿAmmār, Khabbāb, and others like them from the weak Muslims. They said: ""O Muḥammad! Drive them away. Are you pleased with these from your people? Shall we be followers of these?! Are these the ones upon whom Allah has favored from among us?! Perhaps if you drive them away, we will come to you!"" So there was revealed: ""And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face. You are not accountable for them in anything, nor are they accountable for you in anything, that you should drive them away and thus be among the wrongdoers."" (Al-Anʿām: 52)"
"2199","On the authority of Ibn ‘Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away [from this world], and he had appointed Abu Ruhm Kulthum ibn al-Husayn al-Ghifari as his successor over Medina. He set out for ten nights remaining in Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted, and the people fasted with him until, when he was at Al-Kadid — between ‘Usfan and ‘Amj — he broke his fast. Then he proceeded until he alighted at Marr al-Zahran with ten thousand Muslims from Muzaynah and Sulaym, and in every tribe there were numbers and Islam. The Emigrants (Muhajirun) and the Helpers (Ansar) accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in full force; not a single one of them stayed behind. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighted at Marr al-Zahran, the news had been cut off from Quraysh; no news of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached them, and they did not know what he was going to do. On that night, Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Hakim ibn Hizam, and Budayl ibn Warqa’ went out, scouting and waiting to see if they could find any news or hear of it. Al-‘Abbas ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib had already come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ along the way. Also, Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib and ‘Abdullah ibn Abi Umayyah ibn al-Mughirah had met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ between Mecca and Medina. They sought permission to enter upon him. Umm Salamah spoke to him concerning them, saying: ""O Messenger of Allah! Your cousin, your aunt’s son, and your brother-in-law."" He said: ""I have no need for them. As for my cousin, he violated my honor; and as for my aunt’s son and my brother-in-law, he is the one who said to me in Mecca what he said."" When this reply was conveyed to them, and Abu Sufyan had a young son with him, he said: ""By Allah, he will surely give me permission, or I will take this son of mine by the hand, and we will wander the earth until we die of thirst and hunger."" When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he felt compassion for them, then gave them permission. They entered and embraced Islam. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighted at Marr al-Zahran, Al-‘Abbas said: ""Morning has come for Quraysh! By Allah, if the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enters Mecca by force before they seek quarter, it will be the destruction of Quraysh forever."" He said: ""So I sat on the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ white mule and rode out on it until I reached Al-Arak. I said: 'Perhaps I will meet some woodcutters, or a milk carrier, or someone with a need who will go to Mecca and inform them of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ location, so they may come out to him and seek quarter before he enters it upon them by force.' By Allah, I was traveling on it, seeking what I had gone out for, when I heard the speech of Abu Sufyan and Budayl ibn Warqa’, as they conversed with one another. Abu Sufyan was saying: 'I have never seen fires or an army like this day.' Budayl said: 'These are, by Allah, the fires of Khuza’ah, stirred up by war.' Abu Sufyan said: 'Khuza’ah, by Allah, are too humiliated and base for these to be their fires and their army.' I recognized his voice, so I said: 'O Abu Hanzalah!' He recognized my voice and said: 'Abu al-Fadl?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'What is the matter, may my father and mother be ransomed for you?' I said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the people. Morning has come for Quraysh, by Allah!' He said: 'What is the remedy, may my father and mother be ransomed for you?' I said: 'By Allah, if he captures you, he will surely strike your neck. Ride behind me on this mule until I bring you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to seek quarter for you.' So he rode behind me, and his two companions returned. Whenever I passed a fire of the Muslims, they would say: 'Who is this?' When they saw the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ mule, they would say: 'The uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on his mule.' Until I passed the fire of ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: 'Who is this?' and stood up toward me. When he saw Abu Sufyan on the crupper of the camel, he said: 'Abu Sufyan? The enemy of Allah? Praise be to Allah Who has given you power over him without a contract or covenant.' Then he rushed off toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I spurred the mule. It outpaced him by the measure that a swift beast outpaces a slow man. I dismounted from the mule and entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and ‘Umar entered. He said: 'O Messenger of Allah! This is Abu Sufyan; Allah has given you power over him without a contract or covenant. Let me strike his neck.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have [already] granted him protection.' Then I sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, took hold of his [Abu Sufyan’s] head, and said: 'By Allah, no man shall converse privately with him tonight except me.' When ‘Umar persisted in his regard, I said: 'Gently, O ‘Umar! By Allah, if he were from the men of Banu ‘Adi ibn Ka’b, you would not have said this. But you know he is a man from the men of Banu ‘Abd Manaf.' He said: 'Gently, O ‘Abbas! By Allah, your Islam on the day you embraced Islam was dearer to me than the Islam of al-Khattab would have been had he embraced Islam. And that is only because I knew that your Islam was dearer to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than the Islam of al-Khattab [had he embraced Islam].' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Take him to your quarters, O ‘Abbas! When morning comes, bring him to me.' So I took him to my quarters, and he spent the night with me. When morning came, I went out with him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! Has the time not come for you to know that there is no deity but Allah?' He said: 'May my father and mother be ransomed for you! How generous, forbearing, and kind you are! By Allah, I thought that if there were another with Allah, he would have availed me something [by now].' He said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! Has the time not come for you to know that I am the Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'May my father and mother be ransomed for you! How forbearing, generous, and kind you are! By Allah, there was still something in my soul concerning this until now.' Al-‘Abbas said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! Embrace Islam and testify that there is no deity but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah before your neck is struck.' So he testified with the testimony of truth and embraced Islam. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Abu Sufyan is a man who loves this pride/honor; so grant him something.' He said: 'Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe; whoever closes his door is safe; and whoever enters the Mosque is safe.' When he turned to leave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'O ‘Abbas! Detain him at the narrow pass of the valley by the mountain’s edge, until the soldiers of Allah pass by him so he may see them.' So I went out with him and detained him where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded me. The tribes passed by him on their banners. Every time a tribe passed, he would say: 'Who are these?' I would say: 'Sulaym.' He would say: 'What have I to do with Sulaym?' Then another tribe would pass. He would say: 'Who are these?' I would say: 'Muzaynah.' He would say: 'What have I to do with Muzaynah?' Until the tribes had all passed. No tribe passed but he would say: 'Who are these?' I would say: 'Banu so-and-so.' He would say: 'What have I to do with Banu so-and-so?' Until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed in the Green Battalion (Al-Khadra’), in which were the Emigrants and the Helpers. Nothing of them could be seen but their eyes. He said: 'Glory be to Allah! Who are these, O ‘Abbas?!' I said: 'This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the Emigrants and the Helpers.' He said: 'No one has any power or strength against these. By Allah, O Abu al-Fadl! The kingdom of your brother’s son has become great this morning.' I said: 'O Abu Sufyan! This is Prophethood.' He said: 'Then yes, indeed.' I said: 'Go to your people.' So he went out until, when he came to them, he cried out at the top of his voice: 'O people of Quraysh! This is Muhammad, come to you with what you cannot resist. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe.' His wife, Hind bint ‘Utbah, stood up to him, seized his moustache, and said: 'Kill the greasy, fat one! What a wretched scout for a people!' He said: 'Woe to you! Do not let this woman deceive you from yourselves. He has come with what you cannot resist. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe.' They said: 'Woe to you! What good is your house?' He said: 'And whoever closes his door is safe, and whoever enters the Mosque is safe.' So the people dispersed to their homes and to the Mosque."""
"2200","[AI-translation] Khudri who said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) was one day distributing wealth, Dhu al-Khuwaysirah, a man from Banu Tamim, came to him and said: ""O Muhammad! Be just, for by Allah, you have not been just today!"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""By Allah, you will not find after me anyone more just to you than me,"" three times. 'Umar said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you permit me to strike his neck?"" He said: ""No, for he has companions, one of whom would belittle his prayer compared to their prayer..."" [the hadith]"
"2201","[AI-translation] The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) was born in the Year of the Elephant. It is narrated from the hadith of 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas and Qays ibn Makhramah, may Allah be pleased with them."
"2202","[AI-translation] From Umm Hani' bint Abi Talib from her, she said: When it was the day of the conquest of Makkah, I granted protection to two men who were my in-laws. I brought them into a house and locked the door on them. My maternal brother 'Ali ibn Abi Talib came, unsheathing his sword against them. She said: I went to the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) but did not find him, and found Fatimah, who was harsher on me than her husband. She said: The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) came, and upon him were traces of dust. I informed him, and he said: ""O Umm Hani', we have granted protection to those you have granted protection, and we have given security to those you have given security."
"2203","(2203) Rabi'ah al-Aslami narrated: ""I used to serve the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), so he gave me a piece of land, and he gave Abu Bakr a piece of land. Then worldly matters intervened, and we disputed over a palm tree cluster. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'It is within the boundary of my land,' and I said: 'It is within my boundary.' There was an exchange of words between me and Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr said a word to me that I disliked, and he regretted it. He said to me: 'O Rabi'ah! Say the same to me in return so that it may be retaliation.' I said: 'I will not do it.' Abu Bakr said: 'You will surely say it, or I will seek the Messenger of Allah's judgment against you.' I said: 'I am not doing it.' He said: 'And he gave up the land.' Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) went to the Prophet (peace be upon him), and I followed him. Some people from Aslam came and said: 'May Allah have mercy on Abu Bakr! For what reason does he seek the Messenger of Allah's judgment against you when he is the one who said to you what he said?' I said: 'Do you know who this is? This is Abu Bakr as-Siddiq, and he is the second of the two (in the cave), he is the elder of the Muslims. Beware! He may turn and see you supporting me against him, then he will become angry, then he will go to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) who will become angry for his anger, then Allah will become angry for their anger, and Rabi'ah will be destroyed.' They said: 'What do you command us?' He said: 'Return.' Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) went to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and I followed him alone, and I kept following him until he came to the Prophet (peace be upon him). He narrated the incident to him as it had happened. He raised his head to me and said: 'O Rabi'ah! What is between you and as-Siddiq?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, such and such happened: he said a word to me that I disliked, and he said to me: ""Say to me as I said to you so that it may be retaliation."" [So I refused]?' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: 'Yes, so do not say it back to him, but say: ""May Allah forgive you, O Abu Bakr!""' And it is added: ['I said: ""May Allah forgive you, O Abu Bakr!""'] He said: 'Then Abu Bakr turned away, may Allah have mercy on him, weeping.'"""
"2204","[AI-translation] Sakuni that when the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) sent Mu'adh out, the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) went out with him to advise him, Mu'adh riding and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) walking beneath his mount. When he finished, he said: ""O Mu'adh! Perhaps you will not meet me after this year of mine, and perhaps you will pass by my mosque and my grave."" Mu'adh bin Jabal wept in grief due to parting from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family). The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not weep, O Mu'adh! Indeed, weeping is from Shaytan."
